            'title' => 'What exists when nothing exists',
            'image_alt' => 'A closed leather book and an empty glass on a wooden table by a window, violet and teal light, a blurred dialysis ward and a thin moon beyond the glass.',
            'excerpt' => 'On Sunday 27 September 2026 I finished an introduction to metaphysics. This essay is not a shortened textbook. It is what remains after checking: Sunday is not the seventh day, Libet’s paper is 1983, and a bridge between the academy and esotericism must not erase the difference in method.',
            'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>On Sunday 27 September 2026 I finished an introduction that sits in the library under the title <a href="library.php?slug=metafyzika">Metafyzika</a>. The first page carries a sentence: “What exists when nothing exists?” I take it as a question about being at the moment when being cannot be replaced by anything else. It is not a party riddle. It is a question cultures asked before they had a laboratory. I asked it as a nephrologist, after a week in which “being here” meant pressure, volume, consciousness and a signature.</p>
<p>This essay is not a shortened textbook of that text. The survey from Plato to integral theory stays in the library, to be read, downloaded and printed. What remains here, once the tables become reading, is why metaphysics cannot be done between two shifts, where the academic and esoteric traditions actually touch, where they part, and which sentences of the Sunday text do not survive a check as they were written.</p>
<h2>Sunday is not the seventh day</h2>
<p>The Sunday text says that Sunday is the seventh day of the Christian calendar, a day of rest and contemplation. The useful core is there, and the count is wrong. In the Jewish week the seventh day is Shabbat, Saturday. Christian Sunday is the Lord’s day, the first day in the count of the week. Rest moved. The number did not.</p>
<p>What remains of the sentence fits practice. Metaphysics asks what exists, how we know it, whether consciousness is more than a side-effect of matter, and whether it makes sense to speak of free will. This is not answered by a blood sample or an odds ratio. It wants slow thought. A dialysis ward will not give that between two shifts. Sunday, as a day the tradition set aside for looking without immediate use, does.</p>
<p>Aristotle called this work first philosophy: a science of being as being, not of one stretch of reality. The writings later named metaphysics were placed, by tradition, after the books on nature, <em>ta meta ta physika</em>, “what comes after the physics.” The story that Andronicus of Rhodes arranged them that way is a tradition, not something the conclusion needs to stand on. The conclusion is elsewhere. Physics asks what things are like. Metaphysics asks what it means that they are at all.</p>
<p>A physician asks what the mechanism of a disease is. The second question is what it means that someone is ill, what health is beyond a value sitting in range, and why that should matter. The first belongs to pathophysiology. A clinician rarely formulates the second. When they do, they are already doing metaphysics. Thomas Aquinas took contemplation as the highest human activity, because it looks at being for its own sake, not for use. A physician who never looks at the second question remains a technician. Technique without that question loses its footing against a reality that closes in.</p>
<h2>The patient is the bearer, anuria is a property</h2>
<p>Ontology does not ask only what exists physically. It asks what exists in any sense: physically, mentally, mathematically, morally, as a possibility. In the <em>Categories</em> Aristotle distinguishes ten highest genera. The Middle Ages, Aquinas and Duns Scotus, kept from this a distinction the clinic knows even when it does not name it. A substance exists on its own. An accident exists only in it. When I say a patient is anuric, I am speaking of a bearer and a property. When the anuria resolves, the patient remains. The property changed, not the one who bears it.</p>
<p>So it is not indifferent whether “disease” is something or only a name. If universals are only names, as Ockham held, medicine is a bundle of techniques without a unifying concept. If disease is only the shadow of an idea, as in Plato, the clinician treats the shadow and never sees the idea. The moderate Aristotelian version, that the universal is in the particular, is the one Monday can work with. It does not require certainty. It requires that the patient not disappear into a parameter.</p>
<p>David Hume (1711–1776) divided known truths into relations of ideas and matters of fact. Metaphysical sentences such as “the soul is immortal” or “the world has a cause” fell into neither bin, so he threw them out. Immanuel Kant (1724–1804), in the <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em> (1781), did not abolish metaphysics. He moved it. For him it is possible as a critique of reason, a map of the limits of experience, not as a report on the thing in itself. The antinomies reason falls into when it crosses the limit are, for him, a warning, not a result. That turn still defines academic metaphysics. On the ward a more modest sentence is enough: a parameter is a phenomenon. The bearer is not a parameter.</p>
<h2>When the patient is still here</h2>
<p>Thomas Nagel, in <em>What Is It Like to Be a Bat?</em> (1974), named what cannot be handed over from the third person: that there is something it is like to be this creature. In 1995 David Chalmers split the problem of consciousness in two. The easy problems are hard, but they are about mechanism: how attention binds, how a percept is assembled, how a system behaves. The hard problem is different. Why is processing accompanied by there being something it is like for someone. Why we are not philosophical zombies, systems that would pass from the outside while inside there was darkness. In 1983 Joseph Levine called the gap between “this fibre fired” and “it hurts” the explanatory gap. Another experiment will not close the gap. It changes the description of the correlate, not the feeling.</p>
<p>In 2004 Giulio Tononi proposed the integrated information theory. In it, consciousness corresponds to integrated information, the quantity Φ, what a system as a whole carries beyond the sum of its parts. The theory is ambitious twice over. It gives a mathematical definition and shifts consciousness toward being a property of a certain structure, not only a side-effect. That brings it near panpsychism. It is not a proof. It is a programme.</p>
<p>For a nephrologist this is not a curiosity. Coma, limited consciousness, a dialysis patient whose cognition is narrowing: when are they still here, and when does the report describe a reflex. A neurological finding can be a correlate of consciousness and a correlate of activity that carries none. This sentence is not a protocol and not a threshold for withdrawal. It is a reason why, on a Sunday, it is worth knowing that “is here” is not a laboratory item.</p>
<h2>Consent is not chance</h2>
<p>Free will has three storeys: whether the world is determined, whether we can know that, and whether responsibility remains without freedom. Determinism, from Spinoza to Laplace’s demon, says that given conditions and laws there is one future. Compatibilism, Hobbes, Hume, Dennett, says freedom is acting from one’s own motives even in a world that is determined. Libertarianism in the philosophical sense, not the political one, says responsibility needs an originator who is neither a link in the chain nor a roll of the dice. Peter van Inwagen and others can name this. They cannot store it in physics without a remainder.</p>
<p>Quantum mechanics takes Laplace’s single future and gives a distribution. A random event is not a free event. Between the determined and the random a gap remains, which the clinic uses anyway: a person who can consent and can refuse.</p>
<p>In 1983, in the journal <em>Brain</em>, Benjamin Libet and colleagues showed that the brain’s readiness potential precedes the moment a person reports a conscious urge. The Sunday text says 1980. The paper that entered the dispute is from 1983. Libet did not conclude that consciousness is useless. He proposed that conscious will need not start an action, but can stop it. This was called free won’t. In 2008 Soon, Brass, Heinze and Haynes, in <em>Nature Neuroscience</em>, pushed a similar lead, for a simple choice of left or right, to as much as ten seconds and into imaging. The dispute is not closed. Later work has asked whether the readiness potential is a signal of decision at all, rather than an artefact of averaging. I will not let the experiment steal consent. I will keep from it the question metaphysics ought to hold open: the conscious urge need not be the first movement in the head, and yet it is what the sentence “I decided” stands on.</p>
<p>In the piece on <a href="article.php?slug=dobry-skeptik-viac-nez-pochybnost">a good sceptic</a> I wrote that a view should be firm enough to act on and loose enough to revise. A patient’s consent is that kind of act. It is not a proof of libertarianism. It is a practice that, without the assumption of an originator, would become a technique performed on a body.</p>
<h2>A bridge that does not erase the difference</h2>
<p>The split between academic and esoteric metaphysics is modern. Plato’s Academy was a school and a community. Plotinus, usually placed roughly between 205 and 270, composes being as a descent from the One through intellect and soul to matter, and at the same time describes the way back. The <em>Corpus Hermeticum</em>, placed in the early centuries of our era, adds gnosis: knowledge that is meant to change the one who knows. Academic metaphysics treats this as foreign. It is not obliged to treat it as a mistake.</p>
<p>The Sunday text marks points where the traditions touch without pretending to be the same thing. Ideas and universals. Consciousness as something more basic than a side-effect, in Galen Strawson and Philip Goff on one side, in the Hermetic “all is mind” on the other. Law and cause, Leibniz’s sufficient reason beside “laws of creation.” Hierarchy and the arising of the higher from the lower. Intuition, which the academic line knows as intellectual insight and the esoteric line as an inner light.</p>
<p>And there are three partings a bridge must not erase. The first is method. An academic sentence should be open to attack by argument. An inner light cannot be handed to another person as a measurement. The second is the threshold of acceptance for reincarnation and existence before death. The esoteric tradition takes them as part of the picture. The academic tradition may examine them and must not swallow them because they fit someone inwardly. The third is purpose. Esoteric metaphysics is operative: knowledge is meant to change a person. Academic metaphysics is theoretical: knowledge may stand on its own. Plato would have said that true philosophy wants both. That is not a reason to erase the difference. It is a reason to name it.</p>
<p>From this survey I take three stops, not a catalogue. The others, Rudolf Steiner, George Gurdjieff, Sri Aurobindo, Ken Wilber, are in the library, so they can be read whole and not as a slogan.</p>
<p>The <em>Kybalion</em> appeared in 1908 under the name of the Three Initiates. Scholars often identify the author with William Walker Atkinson. The seven principles are a modern esoteric reading, not a text from an Egyptian archive. Mentalism, “all is mind,” is a form of idealism of the kind George Berkeley knew, and in another version a part of today’s panpsychism. Correspondence, “as above, so below,” can bear a structural analogy. It cannot bear the identity of microcosm and macrocosm. I will not present vibration as quantum mechanics or string theory under another name. A parallel may illuminate. It is not a result.</p>
<p>The Sunday text ties the Grail Movement to Abd-ru-shin, Oskar Ernst Bernhardt (18 April 1875 to 6 December 1941). <em>The Grail Message</em> appeared first in 1926, with an expanded edition in 1931. What is interesting is the nomological skeleton: unchanging laws, the weight of an act that returns, the image of a spiritual gravity. That can be compared with causality. It cannot be swallowed merely because the skeleton fits. Its epistemic status hangs on an inner light. For an academic reader that is too little. For a Neoplatonist it would be natural. Both sentences can be true at once.</p>
<p>David R. Hawkins (1927–2012), in <em>Power vs. Force</em> (1995), draws a map of states from shame to what he calls enlightenment, and distinguishes power, which unifies, from force, which needs resistance. As a description of states the map can be compared with developmental stages of morality. The method by which he wants to measure states, applied kinesiology and calibration by a muscle test, is marked in the Sunday text as the weakest point of the whole survey: it verifies itself and does not hold up as measurement. I take it that way. The map may remain as a phenomenological description. A number on a scale is not a measurement until the method survives someone else’s test.</p>
<p>The bridge I take from Sunday is phenomenology. Husserl’s “to the things themselves” asks first how it appears, and only then which doctrine may grow from that. Esoteric experience is then neither binned in advance nor turned into dogma. Academic metaphysics can return to the seriousness it had when it was also a path, not only a technique of argument. The esoteric tradition can learn to separate experience, interpretation and dogma. The difference need not be abolished. It needs to be walked.</p>
<h2>What I do not take from Sunday as certainty</h2>
<p>Four corrections belong in the record, not in shame. Sunday is not the seventh day of the Jewish week. The Libet paper that opened the dispute is from 1983, not 1980, and its reading remains disputed. Hermetic “vibration” is not quantum theory under another name. The sentence “philosophy begins in wonder,” which the Sunday text attributes to Socrates, is in Plato’s <em>Theaetetus</em> (155d) in Socrates’ mouth, and Aristotle opens the <em>Metaphysics</em> with the same thought. Socrates did not write it into a book. Wonder as a beginning remains.</p>
<p>At the start of the twentieth century metaphysics looked dead, because logical positivism declared sentences that could not be checked to be empty. Science has since produced them itself: the measurement problem, consciousness, the question why there is something rather than nothing. That does not revive metaphysics as queen of the sciences. It revives it as a discipline without which interpretation stays silent.</p>
<p>I leave the conclusion of the Sunday text standing, after the corrections. Metaphysics is meant for Sunday, because it asks what the other disciplines assume. The bridge is not a defence of esotericism and not a ban on it. It is an attempt to say where the traditions touch and where they do not. Knowing that there is something we do not know, and that it is worth knowing, is a beginning. Wisdom cannot be looked up. It can only be cultivated.</p>
<p>If you would rather not spend a Sunday like that alone, write to me via the <a href="contact.php">contact form</a>.</p>
<p><em>This is a reading of my own text of 27 September 2026, and of how I use it. It is not medical advice, not a recommendation of esoteric practice, and not a guide to judging consciousness at the bedside. A clinical decision belongs to medicine, not to this essay. The corrections of dates and of the calendar are corrections of the Sunday text.</em></p>
